ber the gunner's  name they'll come to me and we were  sitting up on the flight deck  around six o'clock in the morning now  most of the guys were smarter than me  and that's not impossible most of them  are always smarter than me and they went  to bed  they said screw this instead of hanging  around they went to bed so we were just  walking on the flight deck back and  forth and a guy comes running up and he  says we got a problem we got 11 marines  left on the roof at the embassy  and i looked at him of course saying  sure you do  this this can't happen but they said you  got to get that aircraft there was one  aircraft on the flight deck they said go  get it  and go  and so  the crew chief stan hughes i said is the  aircraft ready to go he said yeah so he  got it buttoned up and everything  steve cook got in there i got in there  and away we went  and we were sort of numb  literally numb because we were so tired  and we started going inbound  just like this aircraft here was a  yankee tank with 22 callsign swift  and  the problem was  over there along the coast you get these  squall lines you get thunderstorms that  just pop up and they go up about 15 000  feet or higher  and we thought oh boy how are we going  to get through there we knew that the  line might be  half mile to a mile wide  and we were trying to think about back  at flight school what they tell us about  penetrating squall lines and  thunderstorms and we finally made a  point that we were going to go through  it  which we did and  the hail in there was so hot bad  the h-46 has windshield wipers which i  always thought was a nuisance but the  hill was so bad that it blew them off  the aircraft  i don't know where they ever ended up  and we but we did break out it was about  a half a mile  and we used so much fuel doing that i  knew fuel was going to be a problem so  we went straight ahead went up the  saigon river  and stayed right about 500 feet saw the  rooftop came in landed picked up the 11  marines and that's where something else  interesting happened um  there's several books written about this  and most the books embellish a whole  bunch of stuff  and i was called one time and they asked  questions  and i couldn't remember very much and i  was trying to they asked me well you  they say you  landed you picked them up and then you  landed again and your crew chief says  that was because you had to get your gas  mask on because the marines have popped  their cs gas containers to hold off the  vietnamese trying to get to the roof  and i thought boy that doesn't sound  right because i don't think i ever  checked out a gas mask  but i didn't think about it very much  and i let it go  there's a there's a i told one fella  here i said there's a great  video out  by the american experience pbs american  experience called the last days  and i'm watching it  and son of a gun what happened was  when we landed we picked up the 11 guys  but the last guy getting on an aircraft  over the ramp in the back fell out  and so when we got up about five or six  feet off the ground getting ready to go  the lead leader of that team yelled at  the crew chief to yell at me to say no  sit it back down which we did and that  guy finally crawled back on the aircraft  so we had all 11 on board so that  answered a question that had been  burning in my mind for years  and then the other thing that burned in  my mind  a lot of you probably see it maybe you  see it at the exhibit here it shows a a  huey  sitting on a small  rooftop  with a whole line of people coming up to  it and getting on it  that's sort of an iconic picture  but that is a air america c.i.a huey  and that's on top of a apartment  building and he's taking his family and  his wife's family  out of the country with them  so that's what that's all about and some  other air america people landed there to  get other people out of that apartment  house  but  that was usually several days before the  final evacuation  anyway we lifted off and we headed down  the saigon river  and  that day was a beautiful sunny day  and everybody was along the riverbanks  waving like this to us you know of  course we put our hands out and waved  back at them  and i got to thinking this is really  surreal this couldn't be happening but  they were just seemed to be so calm  and no one shot at us they could have  because when we landed  the embassy was literally surrounded by  the t-72 tanks  and it was surrounded by the  their aaa and aircraft guns in the back  of these trucks and they tracked us in  and out and of course told the gunner  don't ever don't even think about  shooting at them because we don't want  to make them mad  but uh i knew then there was something  strange going on about the rules of  engagement  anyway we flew back got to the ship got  to the uss okinawa  we left the rooftop at about 7 53 in the  morning  and got back to the okinawa at about 8  20 refueled went over to the hancock  this time i made sure that i went to bed  i didn't hang around i just said  everybody get out of here go hide  someplace  and we did  that night over a thousand 54 hours were  flown by the air crews  682 sorties that's in and out  the evacuation of the dao the defense  attaches compound over at tonsinod air  force base  they took out 395 americans  4  475 vietnamese  went out  at the u.s  third countries lifted out of the  embassy  978 americans came out of the embassy  and third country nationals and another  1  120 vietnamese  so there was a total of 1300 plus  americans evacuated on that mission  5595  vietnamese  and third country nationals  ending up in the united states was over  138  000 vietnamese from that from the  evacuation by sea air and across the  border into thailand  the sad part is we did lose our search  and  search rescue aircraft with captain  nystal and lieutenant shea  i wish that could have been different  the irony there was if there is one  bill nystal had just shown up in okinawa  and  there were some people that just didn't  come he knew we were short on pilots  his wife and kids had just showed up in  okinawa  and we told him to stay behind but he  came and then that night the question  was what mission do we give this guy  because we had no idea what the outcome  of this operation would be  and we said the search and rescue  chopper would be the best place  well it turned out that it wasn't  um today i look at some of this stuff  and i'm thinking that um  let me see what we got here  one more here  this was the hanger deck by the way of  the hancock  and let me tell you a good story here  there's a i don't know how many hundreds  of people are there  um  this became a real issue  uh we're going to be at sea for four to  five days on our way back to the  philippines  and you have to feed them and when i  talked i met with admiral wittmeyer  when i was an instructor at the national  war college later in my career  and  we were sitting down talking  and i asked them what's the most  important thing you thought about out  there you know like  people shooting at us do we have enough  military aircraft we can this and this  he said no he said i was worried about  water cups and food  and i looked at him i said what do you  mean  he says these people have to eat the  same diet  they've been eating i can't afford to  give them steak and ice cream and  everything we eat  i need rice all the rice i can get  and then he had people every every ship  had coca-cola machines  and every can was kept the empty can and  cut in half and used for a cup so when  you got off a helicopter coming on board  the ship you were frisked  for any contraband  you were given a cup  and then we had the galley set up a  special galley for these folks of rice  and a few other items that they were  familiar with because you had to do that  and that was one of his biggest concerns  one of the other great concerns was  what do you do with them for five days  the engineers naval engineers on the  ship have my greatest respect  because one of the problems is  you have to have a way to relieve  yourself  all those people  and they went out on the hangar  elevator that goes up and down and they  built these  miraculous latrines  i guess you can call them for men and  women  separately  and it was quite an invention that they  came up with but they were able to solve  that problem  but then again you had boredom  and  captain eagle  a ch-53 pilot one of my favorites  he came up to me and he said  well i got an idea  now eagle was known as the tuba man  and ray kroc owned the  san diego padres  and at the first game of the first of  the season  tuba man always had to be there  because he played the tuba  for ray kroc  and his tuba was a bright green  camouflage in some places  and he went and got the tuba  and he would literally  walk up and down the aisle here  and these little kids  they would jump in behind him like a  snake following him  and he did that for about an hour one  day  and he was going to quit  he was getting tired  and the captain of the ship came down  and says  that's really great and he said thank  you he says i'm going to quit and he  says no you're not  he says you're going to be here for the  next six hours  by the time we got to  uh subig bay in the philippines he his  lips  but the the kids just gathered around  him they loved him and he really saved  the day  but that was quite a mess there  swift 2-2 where are you today i just saw  this in a magazine about well less than  a month ago this was the famous aircraft  i flew in and out with  it's at davis mothen at the boneyard  uh it's in a cocoon i'm i'm glad to tell  you that  that aircraft is going to be uh  soon to be relocated to the castle air  museum in atwater california  they're going to repair it and put it  back into  original colors and everything and it'll  be a museum piece  but that's where it is today and that's  how it looks  that's how i feel someday when i get up  so uh  oh yeah one last epitaph  uh when we came back we got off they  kicked us off the hancock the hancock  brought their a4s back on their a7s they  were glad to see helicopters go because  it's a real insult to a  fixed wing carrier to have helicopters i  mean they just  my god how low can you go  and uh they were just so happy to see us  leave so we left and we went down and  landed at uh qb point beautiful air  station in the philippines and  we needed to get back to okinawa i got  14 aircraft crews and i'm working  different angles what ship is heading  back that way we can get on and  get home  we got on the okinawa  we're sailing back to uh  okinawa the island  on the uss okinawa  where we got the aircraft spotted i'm in  the lead aircraft  the rotors are turning everything's good  i can see on the horizon  the  silhouette of the island coming up and  at that time  as some of you know me i had a little  girlfriend there and i was eager to get  back and all of a sudden  as i'm sitting there the ship does one  of these hard 90 degree turns and then  another 90 degree turn out of the wind  and i'm yelling at the crew chief take  the chains off do something we got to  get off this ship and immediately got  the shutdown signal  and a thing called mayaguez had happened  where a bunch of people had taken a u.s  ship freighter  and that's a whole other story but we  could not get back to okinawa  so we were forced to hang out at the  pool at qb point here for 30 days  and  san miguel was 25 cents  the famous qb hot dog was another 25  cents and i can't even begin to tell you  how many hot dogs and san miguel beers i  had for those 30 days  but nobody drowned while i was there  so that was the good news  and uh and that's my story i hope you  enjoyed it if there are any questions  i'd be glad to  answer them  folks as a reminder  tom you're going to get two rounds of  applause tonight folks as a reminder you  are welcome to come to either side of  the stage add a mic or if you're unable  i will come to you  this is my real hair by the way  good  from the audience  tom what was your fuel situation on your  last when you left the embassy going  back to the carrier what was your fuel  situation  desperately low  if you look  at the last day's  video from american experience which was  interesting and they had some pictures  of the last 11 guys in the back of the  aircraft  great picture i don't know how they got  it  and one picture  was forward to the picture of the guys  and you could see in the cockpit the two  warning lights  and the warning lights come on at  roughly 400 pounds of fuel aside  and  the question we had and the sad part is  and i love the navy  maybe  you can't get them to turn a ship around  sometimes  and we called them and we said we were  short on fuel and you better turn around  if you don't want us to go in the water  and good thing it was it was daylight  but we landed with about a hundred  pounds of side we just barely made it  back  to the ship and okinawa was the closest  one that's why we went there first  but yeah fuel was a big problem and it  was a problem during the evening  when we flew  i could tell you one other good little  side story  then i'll take your question as we're  coming back  i got up one morning i had the early  morning uh flight  and again i told you there's a whole  bunch of ships i mean we had the hancock  the midway and all this stuff  and i take off and i'm flying and all of  a sudden i look i see something strange  right in the middle of this fleet  was this cruise ship  how it got there i don't know but  everybody on the cruise ship was out on  the rails  waving and looking and they were  they couldn't have been more than 100  yards away from the midway which is a  great sight to see anyway especially if  you go to san diego today it's a  wonderful museum out there  but these people on a cruise ship are  right in the middle  and i'm thinking how did they get there  because you just can't penetrate that  they had to do it at night somehow and  just odd  and when i went back after that flight  they were gone i don't know how they did  it  okay yeah any other questions  so the marines that had been left behind  at the embassy that you went back and  got was  was that because  i mean people had stopped because they  knew they'd  um you know that tiger was back or like  did you ever learn anymore about how  that something like that could happen  good question i read it i've tried to  read everything i can get my hands on  basically  the whole operation was stopped when  they said tigers out  and then but there were still a few  aircraft flying that made a few last  trips before those marines went into the  embassy locked the doors went up the  stairs to the roof  and  they were up there about an hour and a  half to two hours  and i think they were getting a little  nervous they were calling out to the  ships but again all we had was a uhf  radio and the ships were out to sea  about 60 miles  so getting contact was almost impossible  and  i think when they what happened and i  really can't verify this i've talked to  people is that  on the okinawa uss okinawa  their unit  did a head count  and the question started probably going  around saying where are these guys  and someone said well the last time we  saw him and they were at the embassy and  they couldn't find anybody that could  verify they'd come out so that's how we  sort of  ascertained that that's where they were  you are welcome nasa follow  up have you kept and do you know who  those marines were and have you kept you  know found any of them or  no i've i know who they are  i was told one of them lives here in the  uh i thought in paola but that didn't  turn out like that he didn't and i've  not never been able to  make contact with them i've contacted my  crew chief and the gunner i have not  been able to contact the  co-pilot  but  yeah it'd be nice to it'd be interesting  to talk to them because i'm sure they  could add more  to what i can't remember  of course they're getting my age and  they probably can't remember either  so  our next questions from the audience  toward the back  i'm sure most of us or all of us have  seen the  film that shows people pushing  helicopters off i assume an aircraft  carrier what's the deal with that  uh  when the evacuation or when things were  getting really dicey the south  vietnamese  early on decided they better get out  and  the south vietnamese military had the  hueys  they had chinooks they had some other  private planes and they started flying  and everybody knew we were out there in  the water out in the sea  and they were coming out in small boats  hundreds of boats  and  so they came out to the ships and we had  some space and we landed  some of them  but as it kept growing the numbers were  growing we couldn't keep them all  on the chinooks that were fairly decent  the navy had a crew that would go up  with a saw and cut the rotor blades off  throw the rotor blades in the ocean and  we take the whole  carcass of the aircraft down into the  hangar deck  huey's  the huey aircraft that cia had  we saved a lot of those but it turned  out to be worthless because they were  made by kawasaki industries in japan  and the metal that they used to make it  with the siding and the skin of the  aircraft  could not get an air worthiness  certificate in the united states  it was a co-produced aircraft  so you'll see a lot of pictures where  literally people landed got out and then  they took the aircraft and then they  shoved them off the side of the ship  a couple of things and i think one story  i was told about the  bill nystal who was perished out there  in the aircraft accident  he had spent his day  when they would come out the chinook  would come out drop its family lift up  hover off to the side of the ship and  the pilot  would then  blow his  door off the side of the aircraft and  then he'd sort of stand there and take  the aircraft and push the cyclic this  way and he'd step this way and he'd go  into the ocean  and bill would try to go over and pick  him up the problem we had was a lot of  the vietnamese didn't swim  when they did that and the families told  us that we couldn't find them  that  that would probably be a problem  the other problem we had  is that  we didn't have  navy seals maybe people that could do  water rescue  we had the equipment but we didn't have  people that could enter the water  and so if a helicopter ever comes over  the top of you  the rotor wash coming down on you  literally pushes you underwater  and they said that bill  probably drowned maybe two or three  people that afternoon  and it was weighing heavy on them  and so uh  but yeah that's what happened we  couldn't bring them all back  uh there are some interesting stories uh  what would you know  what kind of souvenir did you bring back  from the war  and a friend of mine said well i brought  a helicopter back  you know and uh he literally did  he uh  the captain of one ship he had they were  pulling these barges  so that they could land these aircraft  out on the barges and when the rescue  was completed the captain told bill and  sublette and another guy okay cut the  barge loose sink it and the navy air  boss on that ship said can you fly that  chinook and bill said certainly and he  said can you fly the huey certainly so  they went out there on the barge did  what they were told got it prepared to  sink and then they flew the aircraft  over to the ship  and so  the captain of the ship thought those  things were left behind in the waters  with the with the barge  well the problem was the captain that  as we were going into civic bay saw  those  and got really irate and told them both  to get those aircraft off this ship so  they did and they went into qb point  well qb point  wasn't going to let them land  because they weren't us aircraft  so they had a real problem and a real  dilemma and finally they landed  the military police arrested him  they took both aircraft away  and parked him the chinook was big  enough we made an office out of it for a  while because to get it back to the  state's bare  transit line the freight  hauler it was too expensive for him to  put it on a ship and bring it back he  didn't have that kind of money  but that's that's true we got rid of a  lot of aircraft  this will be our last question  i'm curious the parallel picture of the  earth side view of the 46  with the foothills in the background  is that laguna coming in and out to  tustin  that's the philippines that is the  philippines okay yeah  beautiful beautiful country to fly over  i don't know if we have any world war ii  guests here tonight that were at leyte  gulf  but leyte golf was one of the most  pristine most beautiful places i've ever  seen from the air you can see 50 feet  straight down in the water  it's just gorgeous  it was beautiful  folks this wraps up our program tom will  be available in the lobby if you have  additional questions as always on behalf  of the national world war one museum  memorial thank you for joining us and  another round of applause for colonel  tom holden  thank you

